MW–M31 High-Fidelity N-Body Framework  
Development Log Book  

---

### Project Goal
Design a research-quality N-body framework in Python/NumPy capable of simulating large-scale galaxy dynamics (e.g., Milky Way–Andromeda merger) with good accuracy and reasonable performance.

Environment: Python + NumPy (Spyder/Jupyter workflow)  
Focus: Numerical stability, physics realism, algorithmic scalability  

---

### Work Completed

1. Framework Architecture
- Planned overall structure of simulation code.
- Selected physical units (kpc, Gyr, Msun) for numerical stability.
- Identified major modules needed:
  - Barnes–Hut tree
  - Multipole expansion
  - Energy tracking
  - Adaptive softening
  - Halo equilibrium sampling

Result: Clear modular structure suitable for research extensions.

---

2. Barnes–Hut Tree Implementation
- Implemented octree node structure.
- Built recursive tree construction algorithm.
- Added center-of-mass calculation.
- Implemented quadrupole tensor to improve force accuracy.

Complexity
- Tree build: ~O(N log N)  
- Memory: ~O(N)  

Result: Efficient force approximation for large particle counts.

---

3. Force Calculation
- Implemented Barnes–Hut opening criterion.
- Added monopole + quadrupole force evaluation.
- Verified correctness against small brute-force tests.

Complexity
- Force evaluation: ~O(N log N) average.

Result: Accurate and scalable gravitational force engine.

---

4. Energy Diagnostics
- Implemented tree-based potential calculation.
- Added total energy computation (kinetic + potential).

Purpose
- Monitor numerical stability and integrator accuracy.

Complexity
- ~O(N log N)

Result: Framework can track conservation laws.

---

5. Adaptive Softening Infrastructure
- Implemented density estimation using nearest neighbors.
- Added softening calculation based on local density.

Complexity
- Current method: O(N²) (prototype stage).

Result: Ready for upgrade to kd-tree neighbor search.

---

6. Eddington Inversion Halo Sampler
- Implemented distribution-function based velocity sampling.
- Ensures halos start in equilibrium.

Result: Realistic galaxy initial-condition support.

---

### Testing & Validation
- Checked dimensional consistency of gravitational constant.
- Verified small-N force calculations vs brute-force results.
- Confirmed tree builds correctly for random particle sets.

---

### Overall Outcome
A well-documented, modular N-body framework capable of supporting research-level galaxy simulations once an integrator and initial conditions are added.

